<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-19" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - Rotation Problem-Solving</title>


  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            A uniform cube of side <m>a</m> and mass <m>M</m> is sliding with speed
            <m>v</m> across a flat horizontal frictionless table when it hits a straight
            very low step perpendicular to <m>v</m>, and the leading lower edge comes
            abruptly to rest.
            Find the minimum speed <m>v</m> for which the cube rolls over after hitting
            the step.
        </p>
        <p>    
            The moment of inertia of a uniform cube of side <m>a</m> and mass <m>M</m>
            rotating about an edge is equal to <m>\frac{2}{3}Ma^{2}</m>.
            Hint: think about the conserved quantities at each step of this process <mdash/>
            before and after hitting the step, and in the turning process.
            The height of the step is negligible.
        </p>

        <image width="50%">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw[fill=gray!40] (0,0) rectangle (1,1) node[pos=0.5]
                    {$M$};
                    \draw[thick] (-1,0)--(3,0)--(3,0.1)--(4,0.1);
                    \node at (-0.25,0.5) {$a$};
                    \draw[-&gt;,&gt;=stealth,ultra thick] (0.2,1.3)--++(0.8,0)
                    node[right] {$v$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
        Consider the contraption shown in the figure, which consists of 2 identical
        masses <m>m</m>, offset from the axis by length <m>\ell</m>; all rods have
        negligible mass.
        The bearings at points A and B constrain our contraption to rotate around
        the axis AB at a fixed angular velocity <m>\vec{\omega}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
        The left diagram shows the view from above; at the moment pictured, all
        segments lie flat in the plane of the page (but as rotation continues, they
        will come out from it).
        A three-dimensional view of the system is shown on the right; everything
        drawn in red is a static support.
        Ignore friction and gravity.
    </p>

    <image source="images\activities\lec19\rotating_crankshaft.png"/>
    <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find the angular momentum of each of the particles, relative to
                    the system center of mass.
                    Sum them to find the total angular momentum of the system, as a
                    vector.
                    Note that <m>\vec{L}</m> is not parallel to <m>\vec{\omega}</m>!
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Show that, in spite of the above fact, <m>L_{z}=I_{z}\omega</m>
                    holds.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    As the rod turns, the component of <m>\vec{L}</m> in the plane
                    for the rod turns with speed <m>\omega</m>.
                    Find <m>\dfrac{d\vec{L}}{dt}</m>.
                    The conclusion here is that there must be a net torque on the
                    rod applied by the supports.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Draw the FBDs for each of the masses and show that the net
                    torque on the masses is equal to <m>\dfrac{d\vec{L}}{dt}</m>, as
                    it should be.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Draw the FBD for the rod AB and, thinking about the torque and
                    force balance, find the direction of the force that the supports
                    exert on the rod (remember that the rod is thin and massless, so
                    it can't have angular momentum or linear momentum).
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
        (Extra, this is KK 7.23) A disk of mass M and radius R unwinds from a tape
        wrapped around it.
        The tape passes over a frictionless pulley, and a mass m is suspended from
        the other end.
        Assume that the disk drops vertically.
    </p>

    <image width="40%" source="images\activities\lec19\disk_drum.png"/>
    <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Relate the accelerations of <m>m</m> and the disk, <m>a</m> and <m>A</m>,
                    respectively, to the angular acceleration <m>\alpha</m> of the
                    disk.
                    Note that the length of tape <alert>does not</alert> remain
                    constant!
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Find a, A, and <m>\alpha</m>.
                    Sanity check: if <m>m=M</m>, then <m>a=A</m> (why?).
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>